# "No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header" — but I set the header on the server

⬆ 244 · Express 4, browser is Chrome 124

I have:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', 'https://app.example.com');
  next();
});
```

GETs work. POSTs with `Content-Type: application/json` fail with the CORS error.

## Accepted answer ⬆ 411 ✓

Your POST is a *preflighted* request. A JSON content type is not in the CORS "simple" set, so the browser sends an `OPTIONS` request first, and your app never answers it — Express falls through to the 404 handler, which doesn't carry your headers.

The preflight response needs three things your middleware doesn't send:

```js
res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET,POST,PUT,DELETE');
res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Headers', 'Content-Type,Authorization');
if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') return res.sendStatus(204);
```

Honestly though, just use the `cors` package rather than hand-rolling it:

```js
import cors from 'cors';
app.use(cors({ origin: 'https://app.example.com', credentials: true }));
```

It handles the OPTIONS short-circuit, `Vary: Origin` (important if anything caches your responses), and the credentials rules.

**On credentials:** if you send cookies, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` is rejected by the browser — you must echo a specific origin *and* set `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. Echo it from an allowlist you control, not blindly from the request's `Origin` header; blind echo plus credentials is effectively no origin restriction at all.

**Edit:** the error message is emitted by the browser, so you will never see it in your server logs. Check the Network tab and look for the OPTIONS row above the failing POST — its status tells you immediately whether the preflight was answered.

# "cannot borrow `self.items` as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable"

⬆ 165

```rust
for item in &self.items {
    if item.stale() {
        self.items.retain(|i| i.id != item.id);
    }
}
```

```
error[E0502]: cannot borrow `self.items` as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable
  --> src/store.rs:48:13
   |
46 |         for item in &self.items {
   |                     -----------
   |                     immutable borrow occurs here
48 |             self.items.retain(|i| i.id != item.id);
   |             ^^^^^^^^^^ mutable borrow occurs here
```

## Accepted answer ⬆ 244 ✓

The compiler is protecting you from a real bug, not being fussy: mutating a `Vec` while iterating it can reallocate the backing buffer and invalidate the iterator. C++ lets you do this and it's undefined behaviour.

For this specific case you don't need the loop at all — `retain` already iterates:

```rust
self.items.retain(|i| !i.stale());
```

One pass, no borrow conflict, and it's O(n) instead of the O(n²) your version would have been.

If the predicate genuinely needs information gathered in a first pass, do it in two phases:

```rust
let doomed: HashSet<Id> = self.items.iter()
    .filter(|i| i.stale())
    .map(|i| i.id)
    .collect();
self.items.retain(|i| !doomed.contains(&i.id));
```

The immutable borrow ends when `collect()` finishes, so the mutable borrow on the next line is fine.

And if you need to *consume* the removed items, `Vec::extract_if` (stable since 1.87) gives you both halves in one pass. Before that stabilized, `drain(..).partition()` was the usual workaround.

Build Your First GraphQL Server

Prerequisites: Node.js 20+, npm, and a terminal.

Step 1 — Initialize

    mkdir graphql-demo && cd graphql-demo
    npm init -y
    npm install graphql @apollo/server

Step 2 — Define a schema

Create src/schema.js:

    export const typeDefs = `#graphql
      type Book {
        id: ID!
        title: String!
        author: Author!
      }
      type Author {
        id: ID!
        name: String!
        books: [Book!]!
      }
      type Query {
        books: [Book!]!
        book(id: ID!): Book
      }
    `;

The exclamation marks mean non-null. Be deliberate about them: making a field non-null is a breaking change to undo later, while making it nullable is not.

Step 3 — Write resolvers

    const books = [
      { id: '1', title: 'The Pragmatic Ledger', authorId: 'a1' },
      { id: '2', title: 'Distributed Enough', authorId: 'a1' },
    ];
    const authors = [{ id: 'a1', name: 'R. Nakamura' }];

    export const resolvers = {
      Query: {
        books: () => books,
        book: (_, { id }) => books.find(b => b.id === id),
      },
      Book: {
        author: (book) => authors.find(a => a.id === book.authorId),
      },
    };

Step 4 — Start the server

Create src/index.js:

    import { ApolloServer } from '@apollo/server';
    import { startStandaloneServer } from '@apollo/server/standalone';
    import { typeDefs } from './schema.js';
    import { resolvers } from './resolvers.js';

    const server = new ApolloServer({ typeDefs, resolvers });
    const { url } = await startStandaloneServer(server, {
      listen: { port: Number(process.env.PORT) || 4000 },
    });
    console.log(`Ready at ${url}`);

Add "type": "module" to package.json, then:

    node src/index.js

Step 5 — Query it

    curl -sS http://localhost:4000/ \
      -H 'content-type: application/json' \
      -d '{"query":"{ books { title author { name } } }"}'

Troubleshooting

SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module. You forgot "type": "module".

A field resolves to null unexpectedly. If the parent object's property name does not match the schema field name, you need an explicit resolver. Apollo's default resolver only does a property lookup.

The N+1 problem: your Book.author resolver runs once per book. With a real database that is one query per row. Introduce a batching data loader before this reaches production.

Write Your First Reusable Terraform Module

Prerequisites: Terraform 1.9+, credentials configured for your provider, and an existing configuration you want to factor into a module.

Step 1 — Create the module layout

    mkdir -p modules/static-site
    cd modules/static-site
    touch main.tf variables.tf outputs.tf README.md

Three files is the convention and it is worth following. Someone reading your module should find inputs in variables.tf without grepping.

Step 2 — Declare inputs with types and descriptions

In variables.tf:

    variable "bucket_name" {
      description = "Globally unique bucket name for the site content"
      type        = string
    }

    variable "index_document" {
      description = "Object key served for directory requests"
      type        = string
      default     = "index.html"
    }

    variable "tags" {
      description = "Tags applied to every created resource"
      type        = map(string)
      default     = {}
    }

Give every variable a type. An untyped variable accepts anything and fails deep inside a resource with an unhelpful message.

Step 3 — Do not configure a provider inside the module

Modules must not contain provider blocks. The root module owns provider configuration; a module that declares its own cannot be used twice with different regions. Declare requirements instead:

    terraform {
      required_version = ">= 1.9"
      required_providers {
        aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws", version = "~> 5.0" }
      }
    }

Step 4 — Expose outputs

    output "website_endpoint" {
      description = "Public URL of the hosted site"
      value       = aws_s3_bucket_website_configuration.this.website_endpoint
    }

Step 5 — Use it from the root module

    module "marketing_site" {
      source      = "./modules/static-site"
      bucket_name = "marketing-site.example.com"
      tags        = { team = "growth", env = "prod" }
    }

Step 6 — Validate and plan

    terraform init
    terraform fmt -recursive
    terraform validate
    terraform plan -out=tfplan

Always plan to a file and apply that file. Applying without a saved plan means the thing you reviewed and the thing that runs are two separate evaluations.

Troubleshooting

"Module not installed" after adding a module block. Run terraform init again; adding a module requires re-initialization.

Every plan wants to recreate a resource. Something in the resource's identity is being recomputed — commonly a name built from a timestamp or a random value without a keeper.

Output is null. You referenced an attribute that is only known after apply, from a context evaluated at plan time.

State lock error after an interrupted run. Confirm nobody else is applying, then force-unlock with the lock id from the error message.
